# Keep generated files out of the source tree. This is useful for the
# usual out-of-tree build reasons, though in this project it gives us
# something a little stronger as well: anything under www/ is input that
# somebody maintains and anything under the corresponding output
# directory is produced by the build.
#
# This makes the output directory a fairly literal image of the website
# we are going to install. In particular, starting with this source
# directory and an empty output directory must be enough to reproduce
# the published site. If we ever find ourselves relying on a generated
# file left in www/, then we have broken that property.

# tailwind.build has already selected the Tailwind artifact for this
# host. At this point we know its URL and checksum, and possibly have a
# configured local copy. What remains is to turn that selection into an
# executable that the rest of this build can depend on.
#
# It may be tempting to acquire Tailwind directly from the site.css
# recipe. Giving the executable its own target is useful here. build2
# can avoid acquiring it until something actually needs it and, perhaps
# more interestingly, the stylesheet gets a real prerequisite
# representing the program that generated it. If the selected Tailwind
# artifact changes, site.css then becomes out of date through the normal
# dependency graph.
#
# Note that this executable belongs to the build machinery. It is
# neither installed with the website nor placed in the distribution. A
# build from a distribution performs the same host selection and
# acquires its own copy.
#
tailwind_cli = exe{tailwindcss}

# Unix Tailwind releases are ordinary files with the executable bit
# expected to be set after downloading. The Windows release already has
# executable semantics in the first place. Record that distinction here
# so the recipe below doesn't have to infer it from the artifact name.
#
tailwind_chmod = ($build.host.class != 'windows')

$tailwind_cli:
{
  install = false
}
{{
  # There are a few inputs here that build2 cannot see in the usual way.
  # Perhaps the obvious one is the URL: changing it may mean a
  # completely different artifact with $> itself unchanged.
  #
  # The checksum is perhaps even more interesting since the URL could
  # stay exactly the same and yet we may now expect different bytes. And
  # then there is $tailwind which, if set, changes where those bytes
  # come from.
  #
  # So let's remember all three.
  #
  depdb string "$tailwind_url"
  depdb string "$tailwind_sha256"
  depdb string "$tailwind"

  # curl can get some of its configuration from the environment. We
  # could probably ignore most of this in the common case, but then
  # changing, say, the certificate setup would be invisible to the
  # recipe. So remember the variables curl may look at as well.
  #
  depdb env SSL_CERT_DIR SSL_CERT_FILE CURL_CA_BUNDLE http_proxy https_proxy no_proxy

  diag tailwindcss $>

  # Now, acquiring directly into $> would be tempting but it gives us a
  # slightly unpleasant case if the operation is interrupted: we could
  # leave what looks like a perfectly good target containing only part
  # of the artifact.
  #
  # So do the acquisition into a temporary file and move it into place
  # once we have checked everything. And since a previous attempt may
  # have stopped before the move, get rid of an old temporary file
  # first.
  #
  t = "$path($>).t"
  rm -f "$t"

  if ("$tailwind" != '')
  {
    # If $tailwind is set then there is nothing to download. Still,
    # let's copy it to the temporary file rather than straight to $>.
    # This way the local artifact goes through exactly the same check
    # below and we only have one path by which $> can appear.
    #
    cp "$tailwind" "$t"
  }
  else
  {
    # Otherwise fetch the selected release artifact. The release URL may
    # redirect, hence --location. A few retries are useful for the usual
    # transient network failures and there isn't much value in curl
    # printing anything when everything went fine.
    #
    $curl --fail        \
          --silent      \
          --show-error  \
          --location    \
          --retry 3     \
          --output "$t" "$tailwind_url"
  }

  # We have something that looks like the artifact now, so check that it
  # is actually the one selected in tailwind.build before going any
  # further.
  #
  sha256sum --sum-only "$t" | set sum

  if ("$sum" != "$tailwind_sha256")
  {
    # Perhaps we could leave the bad file around for inspection, but
    # there is little useful state in it that isn't already captured by
    # the two checksums below. More awkwardly, it would look like
    # unfinished state from the next attempt. So remove it.
    #
    rm -f "$t"

    exit "$tailwind_artifact does not have its expected checksum: wanted \
$tailwind_sha256, got $sum"
  }

  if $tailwind_chmod
  {
    # On Unix the downloaded artifact still needs to be made executable.
    #
    $chmod +x "$t"
  }

  # And now the temporary file is the artifact we wanted and, on hosts
  # where it matters, is executable. So this is the point where we can
  # finally give it the target name.
  #
  mv "$t" $path($>)
}}

# tailwind.css is what we edit and site.css is what the website loads.
# They live at the same relative place in the source and output trees,
# which is convenient, though we still want different names here so the
# authored file and the generated one remain unambiguous to the build.
#
tailwind_in = css{tailwind}

# Tailwind has to scan the HTML before it knows which utility classes to
# emit. The slightly awkward part is that those files are discovered
# through @source in tailwind.css and never appear on the command line
# below. Tailwind knows about them, in other words, but build2 does not.
#
# So list the same files here and make them real prerequisites of
# site.css. There are now two descriptions of the scan set, which is
# perhaps easy to get out of sync. Automatic discovery is disabled in
# tailwind.css and the testscript compares its @source entries with this
# list, so forgetting one side should be caught fairly quickly.
#
tailwind_scan = html{index 404}

css{site}: $tailwind_in $tailwind_cli $tailwind_scan
{
  install = data/www/
}
{{
  diag tailwindcss $>

  # This pipe perhaps deserves an explanation. Tailwind changes the
  # state of the stderr descriptor it inherits. If we hand it the
  # recipe's stderr directly, that change can still be visible to
  # build2's in-process builtins after Tailwind exits.
  #
  # So put a pipe in between and let cat copy the diagnostics back to
  # stderr. Tailwind can then do whatever it does to its end of the pipe
  # without changing the descriptor that build2 keeps using.
  #
  $tailwind_cli --input $path($tailwind_in) --output $path($>) --minify 2>&1 | cat >&2

  # There is one more slightly odd Tailwind detail here. If regenerating
  # the stylesheet produces exactly the same CSS, Tailwind leaves the
  # existing output file alone.
  #
  # Say index.html becomes newer but the edit doesn't add or remove any
  # utility classes. We come through this recipe, Tailwind finds that
  # site.css already has the right contents, and its timestamp remains
  # older than index.html. The next build would then come through here
  # again for no useful reason.
  #
  # So touch the target after a successful run. In the unchanged-content
  # case that simply records that we have already considered the current
  # inputs.
  #
  touch $path($>)
}}

# English is the source language, so there is no gettext work to do for
# it. We still want the language component in the published URL though,
# which means producing en/index.html just like we produce the
# translated pages below.
#
html{en/index}: html{index}
{
  install = data/www/en/
}
{{
  # $url is another input that never appears as a normal prerequisite.
  # If the configured origin changes then this page has to change with
  # it, so remember the value in the depdb.
  #
  depdb string "$url"

  diag gen $>

  # Keep the real production origin in the authored document. That gives
  # index.html sensible metadata when it is opened directly and leaves
  # us with one stable string to replace here.
  #
  # Now substitute the configured origin into the generated copy.
  #
  sed -e "s|https\\://iw4x\\.io|$url|g" $path($<) >$path($>)
}}

pages = html{en/index}

for l: $linguas
{
  p = html{$l/index}
  pages += $p

  # One detail worth getting right here is the catalogue prerequisite.
  # It would be easy to depend on all the PO files and let build2 sort
  # it out, but an edit to fr.po would then make de/index.html out of
  # date for no reason.
  #
  # msgfmt reads exactly one catalogue for each page, so give the page
  # exactly that prerequisite.
  #
  $p: html{index} po{$src_root/po/$l} $its $loc
  {
    install = data/www/$l/
    lang = $l
  }
  {{
    # msgfmt itself was found elsewhere. What we need to remember here
    # is the version we found, since changing it means the program doing
    # this transformation has changed with none of the file
    # prerequisites necessarily changing.
    #
    depdb string "$msgfmt_version"

    # $url and $lang are similar to the Tailwind values above: both
    # affect the result, neither is represented by a file prerequisite.
    # So remember them here as well.
    #
    depdb string "$url"
    depdb string "$lang"

    # msgfmt has one more input that is easy to miss. It finds the ITS
    # data through GETTEXTDATADIR, so a change there is invisible from
    # the command line. Put the environment value into the depdb as
    # well.
    #
    depdb env GETTEXTDATADIR

    diag msgfmt $>

    # The result from msgfmt isn't quite the page we want to publish.
    # There is still a small serialization cleanup below, so keep this
    # first result under a temporary name and reserve $> for the
    # finished page.
    #
    t = "$path($>).t"

    # --replace-text is perhaps easy to overlook. Without it msgfmt
    # keeps the source text and adds the translated text alongside it.
    # Here we want the translated text to occupy the same place as the
    # English text, hence the option.
    #
    env "GETTEXTDATADIR=$gettextdatadir" -- \
      $msgfmt --xml -L iw4x --replace-text "--template=$path($<[0])" \
              --locale=$lang --output-file="$t" $path($<[1])

    # Now comes the slightly fiddly part. msgfmt has done the
    # translation, but its XML serialization is not quite the text/html
    # document we want to publish. There are only a few differences, so
    # fix them together here.
    #
    # First is the prologue. msgfmt writes an XML declaration and keeps
    # the doctype from the template. An HTML parser treats that
    # declaration as a bogus comment, leaving the doctype in the wrong
    # place for what we want. So blank the original doctype and turn the
    # XML declaration into a new one.
    #
    # Note that the order of those two substitutions matters. If we
    # produced the new doctype first, the expression that blanks the old
    # one could see the new one as well. Leaving the old line empty is
    # harmless, so there is no real reason to add another pass just to
    # remove it.
    #
    # Next are the xml:lang attributes that msgfmt adds to translated
    # elements. This page has a single language, so put that information
    # on the root html element and remove the per-element copies.
    #
    # Then fix the canonical URL. The template quite naturally names
    # /en, since that is the page we author. A translated copy needs to
    # name its own language instead.
    #
    # Finally substitute the configured origin. Do this last so the
    # canonical rewrite above can keep matching the stable production
    # URL from the source document instead of having to know what $url
    # happens to be.
    #
    sed -e 's|^\<!DOCTYPE html>$||'                                             \
        -e 's|^<[?]xml[^>]*[?]>$|\<!DOCTYPE html>|'                             \
        -e 's| xml:lang="[a-z]*"||g'                                          \
        -e "s|\<html xmlns=\"http\://www\.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" lang=\"en\">|\<html xmlns=\"http\://www\.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" lang=\"$lang\" xml:lang=\"$lang\">|" \
        -e "s|rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https\://iw4x\\.io/en\"|rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https\://iw4x.io/$lang\"|" \
        -e "s|https\\://iw4x\\.io|$url|g"                                       \
        "$t" >$path($>)

    # And that is the finished page. Nothing below needs the
    # intermediate XML, so get rid of it now rather than leaving scratch
    # state in the output directory.
    #
    rm -f "$t"
  }}
}

# We need the same language alternation in a couple of places below. We
# could construct it independently each time, though that would give us
# two bits of code that are supposed to describe the same language set.
#
# So build it once here. LINGUAS contains the translated languages and,
# as a result, does not contain English. Add it explicitly before
# forming the alternation.
#
locales = $regex.merge(en $linguas, '(.+)', '\1', '|')

# The sitemap has one URL entry for every language page, with each entry
# naming the other language versions of the same document.
#
# It may be tempting to make the generated pages prerequisites here. The
# sitemap never reads them though. All it needs is the language set and
# the origin. Changing a sentence in fr.po cannot change sitemap.xml, so
# depending on html{fr/index} would only make that edit rebuild
# something whose contents are already known to be unchanged.
#
xml{sitemap}:
{
  install = data/www/
}
{{
  # Neither of the two things we actually use to form the URLs is
  # represented by a file prerequisite, so remember them in the depdb.
  #
  depdb string "$url"
  depdb string "$locales"

  diag gen $>

  o = $path($>)

  # Start with the prologue and then append one entry per language.
  # There is no intermediate format here and no external program
  # producing the file for us, so writing the target directly keeps this
  # recipe pleasantly simple.
  #
  echo '<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>' >$o
  echo '<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9" xmlns:xhtml="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">' >>$o

  for l: en $linguas
  {
    echo "  <url>" >>$o
    echo "    <loc>$url/$l</loc>" >>$o

    # Now repeat the full alternate set for this URL. It looks a little
    # repetitive when generated, but each entry has to describe its
    # relation to every other language version, so there isn't really
    # anything to factor out in the resulting XML.
    #
    for a: en $linguas
    {
      echo "    <xhtml:link rel=\"alternate\" hreflang=\"$a\" href=\"$url/$a\"/>" >>$o
    }

    # And use English for the language-neutral entry.
    #
    echo "    <xhtml:link rel=\"alternate\" hreflang=\"x-default\" href=\"$url/en\"/>" >>$o
    echo "  </url>" >>$o
  }

  echo '</urlset>' >>$o
}}

# robots.txt is almost static. The awkward bit is the Sitemap line,
# since its origin follows $url and cannot simply be written once in the
# source tree.
#
# We could add a template for that single substitution. With only four
# lines to produce, writing the file here is perhaps easier to read.
#
file{robots.txt}:
{
  install = data/www/
}
{{
  # $url is the only bit of configuration that can change the result.
  #
  depdb string "$url"

  diag gen $>

  o = $path($>)

  echo 'User-agent: *' >$o
  echo 'Allow: /' >>$o
  echo '' >>$o
  echo "Sitemap: $url/sitemap.xml" >>$o
}}

# The language part of the URL belongs to the site itself, so keep the
# matching server rules here with the rest of the site.
#
# Most of .htaccess is ordinary authored text and lives in htaccess.in.
# The language alternation is the one part that is awkward to write
# there, since it has to follow LINGUAS. So leave a @locales@ hole in
# the template and fill it from the value above.
#
file{.htaccess}: file{htaccess.in}
{
  install = data/www/
}
{{
  # Changing LINGUAS changes $locales without changing htaccess.in, so
  # remember the value explicitly.
  #
  depdb string "$locales"

  diag gen $>

  sed -e "s,@locales@,$locales,g" $path($<) >$path($>)
}}

# 404.html and favicon.ico need no work at all. The @./ qualification is
# worth spelling out though: these are the files in the source
# directory, not targets that we expect some rule to produce in the
# output directory.
#
static = html{404}@./ file{favicon.ico}@./

$static: install = data/www/

# index.html is a slightly different case. It is source for the page
# rules above and should never appear in the installed tree by itself.
# Since html derives from doc, it would otherwise pick up the usual
# installation semantics. Turn those off for this source target.
#
html{index}@./: install = false

# And finally collect the whole site under the directory target.
#
./: css{site} $pages xml{sitemap} $static inter/ \
    file{robots.txt .htaccess}
